Looking at the data sources reference, I'll give you paste-ready HTML using `updatedShoppingCartItems` from the updateCart event.

```handlebars
{{#if updatedShoppingCartItems}}
<table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0">
  {{#each updatedShoppingCartItems}}
    {{#lt @index 3}}
    <tr>
      <td width="120" valign="top">
        <img src="{{imageUrl}}" alt="{{name}}" width="120" style="display:block;">
      </td>
      <td valign="top" style="padding-left:16px;">
        <a href="{{url}}" style="font-weight:bold; color:#000000; text-decoration:none;">{{name}}</a><br>
        Qty {{quantity}} &middot; {{numberFormat price "currency"}}
      </td>
    </tr>
    {{/lt}}
  {{/each}}
  {{#ifGt updatedShoppingCartItems.size 3}}
  <tr>
    <td colspan="2" style="padding-top:16px; font-style:italic; color:#666666;">
      ...and {{math updatedShoppingCartItems.size '-' 3}} more in your cart
    </td>
  </tr>
  {{/ifGt}}
</table>
{{else}}
  <!-- Empty cart fallback or skip send -->
{{/if}}
```

**What it does:** Loops the first 3 cart items with image, linked product name, quantity, and formatted price. If there are more than 3 items, shows a count of the extras.

**About the apostrophes:** The double braces `{{ }}` on `{{name}}` will escape `Levi's 501` as `Levi&#x27;s 501` in the HTML source, which displays correctly as `Levi's 501` in every inbox. That's the right behavior for data values — keep the double braces.

**Data source note:** With a 4-hour delay, you have two cart snapshots available: `updatedShoppingCartItems` (what was in the cart when the event fired) and `profile.shoppingCartItems` (what's in the cart *now* at send time). I used the event version above since you said "triggered off the updateCart event," but if someone adds items during the delay window or checks out, the profile version might be what you want. Confirm which reflects your intended send logic.

**Verify in Preview:** Load a user with a cart, then edit the cart size in Preview to test the 3-item cap and the "and more" line.

